WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.560
"Is It Safe to
Eat Raw Mushrooms?"

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.840
There is a toxin in plain
white button mushrooms

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.880
called agaritine, which
may be carcinogenic.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.240
And plain white button mushrooms
grow up to be cremini mushrooms

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.040
(the brown mushrooms), and cremini
mushrooms grow up to be

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.080
portobello mushrooms—they’re
all the exact same mushroom.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.240
It’s like how green bell peppers
are just unripe red bell peppers.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.280
But you can reduce the amount
of agaritine in these shrooms

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.480
through cooking.
Frying, microwaving, boiling,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.280
or even just freezing and
thawing lowers the levels.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.960
It is therefore recommended to
cook mushrooms before consumption,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.373
something I noted in a video
that’s now more than a decade old.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.280
But if you look at the
various cooking methods

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.920
the agaritine isn’t
completely destroyed.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.160
Take dry baking for example:

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.960
10 minutes at about
400 degrees Fahrenheit

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.880
(basically how you’d make a pizza),
only cuts the agaritine levels

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.040
by about a quarter, so
77 percent still remains.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.760
Boiling looks better,
appearing to wipe out over half

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.840
of the toxin after just five minutes.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.560
But it’s not actually wiped
out; instead, it’s just

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.400
transferred to the cooking water.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.880
So yes, levels within
the mushrooms drop

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.160
by about half at five minutes
and 90 percent after an hour,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.360
but that’s mostly because
it’s leaching into the broth.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.280
So, if you’re making soup or something,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.320
five minutes of boiling is
no better than the pizza,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.840
and even after an
hour about half remains.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.160
Frying for 5 to 10 minutes
wipes out a lot,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.120
but microwaving is a more
healthful way to cook,

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.000
and it works even better.
Just one minute in the microwave

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.120
“reduce[s] the agaritine content
of the [fresh sliced] mushrooms

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.080
by 65 percent,” and only
30 seconds wipes out about half.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.320
So, microwaving is
probably the easiest way

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.600
to reduce agaritine levels
in fresh mushrooms.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.040
What I do with dried mushrooms
is throw them into pasta water

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.480
when I’m making spaghetti and
between the 20-ish percent drop

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.680
from the drying and the 60
percent-ish drop from boiling

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.484
for 10 minutes and straining,
more than 90 percent is wiped out.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.920
Should we be concerned
about the residual agaritine?

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.640
Should we skip mushroom pizza?

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.480
If you’re eating pizza, mushrooms
are probably the last thing

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.240
you need to worry about, but seriously

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.290
should we be concerned
about agaritine?

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.200
According to a review paid
for by the mushroom industry,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.840
not at all. “The available
evidence to date suggests

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.080
that agaritine from consumption
of . . . mushrooms poses

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.680
no known toxicological
risk to healthy humans.”

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.560
The researchers acknowledge
it’s considered a potential

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.320
carcinogen in mice, but
then you have to extrapolate

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.200
that data to human health outcomes.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.320
For example, the Swiss Institute
of Technology estimated

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.360
the average mushroom consumption
in the country would be

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.560
expected to cause about two
cases of cancer per 100,000 people.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.440
That’s actually similar
to U.S. consumption,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.880
so one could theoretically
expect about 20 cancer deaths

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.240
per million lives from
mushroom consumption.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.960
Now typically with a
new chemical, pesticide,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.000
or food additive we’d
like to see less than

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.080
one in a million cancer risk.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.800
By this approach, “the average
mushroom consumption …

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.480
would be 20-fold too
high to be acceptable.”

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.240
To get it down to one in a
million you could only eat

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.840
about a half cup serving
once every 250 days

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.935
or something to remain
under the “tolerable” limit.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.560
But to put that into perspective,
even if you were eating

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.760
a single serving every
single day, the resulting

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.200
additional cancer risk would
only be about 1 in 10,000.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.320
In other words, “if 10,000
people consumed a mushroom meal

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.760
daily for 70 year[s],
then in addition to the

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.560
3,000 cancer cases
arising from other factors,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.280
one more case could be
attributed to consuming mushrooms.”

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.560
But again, this is all
based on the presumption that

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.680
results in mouse models
are valid in humans;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.720
this is all just
extrapolating from mice.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.240
What we need is a huge
prospective study to examine

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.600
the association between mushroom
consumption and cancer risk

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.579
in people, but there weren’t
any such studies until... now.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.920
“Mushroom consumption and
risk of … cancer in [the]

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.866
two large [Harvard] cohorts”, and…

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:31.501
"no association between
mushroom [intake] and [cancer]”.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.840
Eating raw or undercooked
shiitake mushrooms can cause

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:43.040
something else, though: shiitake
mushroom ﬂagellate dermatitis.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.120
Flagellate as in flagellation,
whipping, flogging.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.720
Check out this crazy rash.
You break out in a rash

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.000
that makes it look as if
you’ve been whipped.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.560
Doesn’t it look wild?
That’s just how you break out.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.120
It’s thought to be caused by
a compound in shiitake mushrooms

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.320
called lentinan, but
because heat denatures it,

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.000
it only seems to be a problem
with raw or undercooked mushrooms.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.480
Now it’s rare; only about 1 in
50 people are even susceptible.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.520
And it goes away on its
own in a week or two.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.040
Interestingly, it can strike
as many as 10 days after you

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.360
eat them, which is why people
may not make the connection.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.480
One poor guy suffered on and off
for 16 years before a diagnosis.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.960
Hopefully a lot of doctors
will watch this video,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.800
and if they ever see a rash like this,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.707
they’ll tell their patients
to cook their shiitakes.

